WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.897
Sometimes, bad laws build nations.

2
00:00:02.897 --> 00:00:05.408
Two in particular angered educated

3
00:00:05.408 --> 00:00:06.531
Indians.

4
00:00:06.877 --> 00:00:10.157
Lord Lytton's Vernacular Press Act

5
00:00:10.157 --> 00:00:14.497
of 1878 muzzled Indian-language newspapers.

6
00:00:14.497 --> 00:00:16.668
The Ilbert Bill of 1883

7
00:00:16.668 --> 00:00:19.085
tried to let Indian judges

8
00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.519
try Europeans,

9
00:00:20.519 --> 00:00:23.801
and was diluted after protests.

10
00:00:24.164 --> 00:00:26.639
Western education brought ideas of

11
00:00:26.639 --> 00:00:28.688
liberty. The press and the

12
00:00:28.688 --> 00:00:31.033
railways spread ideas faster.

13
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:32.844
The Ilbert Bill row taught

14
00:00:32.844 --> 00:00:35.001
a lesson: Indians would not

15
00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:36.802
be treated as equals under

16
00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:37.929
the Raj.

17
00:00:38.601 --> 00:00:39.778
Your turn.

18
00:00:39.778 --> 00:00:41.724
What was it about?
